Heap — DSA Notes
A Heap is a complete binary tree that follows a special ordering property called the heap property.

1. Types of Heap
Max Heap
In a Max Heap, every parent is greater than or equal to its children.
        50
       /  \
     30    40
    / \    /
   10 20  35
Root = maximum element

Min Heap
In a Min Heap, every parent is smaller than or equal to its children.
        10
       /  \
     20    15
    / \    /
   30 40  25
Root = minimum element

2. Properties of Heap
A binary heap must satisfy:
It is a complete binary tree.
All levels are completely filled except possibly the last.
The last level is filled from left to right.
It satisfies either:
Max Heap: Parent ≥ Children
Min Heap: Parent ≤ Children
3. Heap Representation Using Array
A heap is usually stored in an array.
For a node at index i using 0-based indexing:
Parent       = (i - 1) / 2
Left Child   = 2*i + 1
Right Child  = 2*i + 2
Example:
Heap:

        50
       /  \
     30    40
    / \    /
   10 20  35

Array:

[50, 30, 40, 10, 20, 35]
4. Insertion in Heap
To insert an element:
Steps
Insert the element at the end of the array.
Compare it with its parent.
If the heap property is violated, swap.
Continue upward until the correct position is found.
This process is called Heapify Up or Bubble Up.
Example — Max Heap
Insert 45:
        50
       /  \
     30    40
    / \    /
   10 20  35
Insert at the end:
        50
       /  \
     30    40
    / \    / \
   10 20  35 45
45 > 40, so swap:
        50
       /  \
     30    45
    / \    / \
   10 20  35 40
Time Complexity
O(log n)
5. Deletion from Heap
Usually, deletion means deleting the root.
Steps
Remove the root.
Move the last element to the root.
Compare it with its children.
Swap with the appropriate child.
Continue downward.
This is called Heapify Down or Bubble Down.
Example
Max Heap:
        50
       /  \
     30    40
    / \    /
   10 20  35
Delete 50.
Move 35 to root:
        35
       /  \
     30    40
    / \
   10 20
Since 40 > 35, swap:
        40
       /  \
     30    35
    / \
   10 20
Time Complexity
O(log n)
6. Heapify
Heapify is the process of restoring the heap property.
There are two types:
Heapify Up
Used mainly after insertion.
Child → Parent
Heapify Down
Used mainly after deletion.
Parent → Child
7. Build Heap
We can convert an unsorted array into a heap.
Example:
[4, 10, 3, 5, 1]
After building a Max Heap:
        10
       /  \
      5    3
     / \
    4   1
Array:
[10, 5, 3, 4, 1]
Time Complexity
O(n)
This is an important exam/interview point:
Building a heap using bottom-up heapification takes O(n), not O(n log n).
8. Heap Sort
Heap can be used for sorting.
For Ascending Order
Use a Max Heap.
Steps
Build a Max Heap.
Swap root with the last element.
Reduce heap size.
Heapify the root.
Repeat.
Complexity
Operation	Complexity
Build Heap	O(n)
Insert	O(log n)
Delete	O(log n)
Get Min/Max	O(1)
Heap Sort	O(n log n)
Search	O(n)
Heap Sort requires O(1) auxiliary space when implemented in-place.
9. Priority Queue vs Heap
A Priority Queue is an abstract data type where elements are processed according to priority.
A Heap is one of the most common data structures used to implement a priority queue.
Priority Queue
      ↓
   Heap
      ↓
Min Heap / Max Heap
10. Heap vs Binary Search Tree
Heap	BST
Complete binary tree	Usually not complete
Root contains min/max	Root doesn't necessarily contain min/max
Searching is O(n)	Average search O(log n)
Insert O(log n)	Average insert O(log n)
Delete root O(log n)	Depends on node
Mainly used for priority queues	Mainly used for searching
Most important point
Heap is not a Binary Search Tree.
In a heap, there is no requirement that:
Left subtree < Root < Right subtree
Only the parent-child relationship matters.
11. Java Example — Max Heap
Java provides PriorityQueue, which by default behaves as a Min Heap.
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
            new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

        maxHeap.add(50);
        maxHeap.add(30);
        maxHeap.add(40);
        maxHeap.add(10);
        maxHeap.add(20);

        System.out.println(maxHeap.peek()); // 50

        maxHeap.poll();

        System.out.println(maxHeap.peek()); // 40
    }
}
Min Heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

minHeap.add(50);
minHeap.add(30);
minHeap.add(40);

System.out.println(minHeap.peek()); // 30
Exam Definition
Heap is a complete binary tree that satisfies the heap property. In a Max Heap, every parent node is greater than or equal to its children, while in a Min Heap, every parent node is smaller than or equal to its children. Heaps are commonly implemented using arrays and are widely used in priority queues and heap sort.
Remember this
MAX HEAP → Maximum at Root
MIN HEAP → Minimum at Root

Insertion → Heapify Up
Deletion  → Heapify Down
Build Heap → O(n)


Heap Sort  → O(n log n)
